Motor neuron disease
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Mixed upper/lower motor neuron signs, Progressive asymmetrical weakness, Widespread muscle fasciculations

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-026
Reason for Visit: Acute respiratory distress and inability to clear secretions in a patient with progressive neurological decline.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and primary caregiver (spouse)
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden, distressing inability to swallow saliva leading to severe coughing fits, choking, and progressive shortness of breath. Urgent in-person evaluation is required to assess airway patency, manage acute aspiration risk, and stabilize respiratory function before the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He can't swallow his own spit anymore, he just chokes and turns blue. His breathing is so shallow tonight."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a known history of progressive, relentless, and distinct asymmetrical limb weakness, which began insidiously in the right hand six months ago. Over the past few weeks, there has been rapidly progressive bulbar involvement resulting in worsening severe dysarthria, profound dysphagia, and a distinctly weak, hoarse voice. Tonight, this has culminated in a severe choking episode on endogenous secretions. The caregiver notes progressive respiratory muscle weakness leading to severe exertional dyspnea, and now orthopnea requiring the patient to sit upright to breathe. The patient exhibits noticeable, progressive head drop strictly secondary to severe, unrelenting weakness of the cervical extensor musculature. The caregiver also reports prominent, widespread, and completely spontaneous muscle fasciculations visibly rippling under the skin of the arms and chest.
Functional Status: Chair-bound; dependent for feeding and all transfers.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of fatal aspiration; caregiver exhaustion and severe distress regarding the patient's breathing.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 mmHg | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 92% on room air | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Sitting upright, distinctly anxious, drooling, using accessory muscles to breathe.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals a coexistence of profound upper motor neuron signs (hyperreflexia at the knees, bilateral Hoffman's sign) simultaneously with lower motor neuron signs (profound atrophy of the intrinsic hand muscles and tongue fasciculations). Widespread muscle fasciculations are noted across the pectoral and deltoid groups. There is strict, undeniable preservation of all sensory modalities and extraocular muscle control. Chest auscultation reveals transmitted upper airway rhonchi from pooled secretions, but bases are clear. Weak, ineffective cough.
Environmental Observations: Suction machine present but caregiver unsure how to operate it effectively.
Medication Reconciliation: Riluzole 50mg BD. Nil acute respiratory medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Motor Neuron Disease (Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis) - Status: Exacerbated (Acute bulbar/respiratory compromise)
Differentials:

Acute Lower Respiratory Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile, clear lung bases without focal crepitations, and presentation primarily driven by upper airway pooling rather than deep pulmonary consolidation.

Myasthenia Gravis Crisis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of profound upper motor neuron signs (hyperreflexia), severe muscle atrophy, and lack of prominent extraocular weakness or fatigable ptosis.

Cervical Myelopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of widespread bulbar signs (tongue atrophy/fasciculations) and diffuse lower motor neuron signs above the level of the cervical spine.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in acute respiratory and bulbar distress due to the rapid progression of Motor Neuron Disease. The inability to clear secretions poses an immediate, life-threatening risk of asphyxiation and aspiration pneumonia. The resting tachypnea and borderline hypoxia indicate impending ventilatory failure. Delaying assessment would risk a fatal respiratory arrest at home. Urgent after-hours assessment allowed for immediate bedside suctioning to secure the airway, the urgent prescription of anticholinergic agents to dry secretions, and the crucial implementation of non-invasive respiratory support strategies, strictly satisfying MBS criteria for an urgent, life-saving intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed immediate deep oropharyngeal suctioning, clearing 20mL of thick saliva. Prescribed Hyoscine Hydrobromide (Scopolamine) 0.3mg sublingually STAT and QID PRN to rapidly dry oral secretions.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By strictly using the suction machine the exact moment a gurgling sound is heard, the caregiver instantly removes the fluid blocking the airway, directly preventing terrifying choking episodes and restoring calm breathing.
Step 2: By keeping the head of the bed elevated at 45 degrees at all times, the patient actively utilizes gravity to keep the diaphragm moving downward, making every breath physically easier and reducing the feeling of suffocation.
Step 3: By administering the drying drops under the tongue exactly 30 minutes before any attempted oral intake, the patient ensures a dry mouth, greatly reducing the risk of aspirating thin fluids into the lungs.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent escalation to the Palliative Care Respiratory Team for Non-Invasive Ventilation (NIV) assessment and formal suction training.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provision of Yankauer suction catheters and replacement tubing.
Follow-up: Community palliative care nurse review tomorrow morning.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call an ambulance immediately if the patient turns blue, becomes unrousable, or if the suction machine fails to clear a severe choking episode.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The disease trajectory involves progressive respiratory muscle weakening; symptom control is the primary medical objective.
Clinical Course: The sublingual drops will significantly reduce the volume of saliva within 60 minutes, easing the breathing. The respiratory team will assess for a breathing mask (NIV) tomorrow to further support the tired chest muscles.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to give the patient thin liquids like water or juice tonight. Use the suction machine as demonstrated.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The muscles in the throat and chest are becoming too weak to swallow normal amounts of saliva or cough it away. The medication we placed under the tongue acts like a sponge, drying up the mouth so he doesn't have to swallow as much. The suction machine acts as an artificial cough to keep the airway safe.
Actionable Steps: Use the suction machine whenever breathing sounds wet. Keep the patient sitting up. Give the drying drops exactly as prescribed.

